#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/id #uuid "a3f9e797-4bfb-5b2a-b460-98d7def3ae20" :question/math-academy-id "e-6754" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta \\le \\pi\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/209/Source/Images/14075e642ddf4d45f6ee40cea7dbb087.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/id #uuid "83fc553b-3ad0-5003-afe3-81204a695162" :question/math-academy-id "e-6755" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3)$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/id #uuid "a2641aac-7c3a-5a2c-97f0-aca4782c7215" :question/math-academy-id "e-7220" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right)$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arcsin x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/209/Source/Images/cbdba1490f8dd76396ad73dfea0fe152.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\n$$\n\nHowever,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\ndoes not lie within the range of $\\theta$. To get an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nand lies within the range of $\\theta$, we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d362583b-059d-5f95-8e73-33e8f2d108e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268992} {:answer/id #uuid "3d69014e-1743-5617-8f77-a7e5eb4f20c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268993} {:answer/id #uuid "08c73ecb-9cab-55d7-a49a-2acc6a650809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268994} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc28348-7f41-527e-9812-7d23328e0dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268995} {:answer/id #uuid "13b11ff9-3ec6-5c0f-946a-01c087ac240e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bc28348-7f41-527e-9812-7d23328e0dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268995} :answer-field/id #uuid "132d2a0e-ea20-5019-adc4-612fc73aac64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268991}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02c3cce7-ac66-5194-ad1f-3ef5e81a3aa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-117411" :question/problem "$-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c189abac-33eb-538f-a4ab-ce03d93bc620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268999} {:answer/id #uuid "cb752986-0b17-5be8-831c-d3a21c158337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269000} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1e074c-9743-514c-964c-e3843d44ce75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269001} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c25ac4-7f5c-5391-814f-8647ed20d95a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269002} {:answer/id #uuid "bea8a93e-8066-5204-898b-584c99326eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c189abac-33eb-538f-a4ab-ce03d93bc620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268999} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2670cb3-d48d-5010-9163-e76ac4205fd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268998}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4591b664-8f5f-503b-bf6e-83fc0b6998fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-117461" :question/problem "$-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3}) & =(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df6bac1f-d8e9-5116-875b-af3f5937d3e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269006} {:answer/id #uuid "a869a4b0-def1-5612-ba46-74eca03db1ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269007} {:answer/id #uuid "5c38c4ee-59e2-5a7c-8290-aa4dc9046baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269008} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4426bc-0611-5b36-b910-1f1a929aef44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269009} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6e582e-88a6-5b88-a68a-2a60ae829c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d6e582e-88a6-5b88-a68a-2a60ae829c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269010} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dded7bc-0e76-5704-885a-26fdcb09c959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e64fd775-535a-57c6-92d2-90544b5aea0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-117462" :question/problem "$4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7330ca0-0ece-5049-85f5-1998cd8bc792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058025} {:answer/id #uuid "e34e91f7-d842-59a5-9ac3-a6c0f25b3659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058026} {:answer/id #uuid "0f073f94-5ce9-55bf-a1f7-afdf27f89321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058027} {:answer/id #uuid "5b838cfd-943d-55b5-b13c-27068314b73c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058028} {:answer/id #uuid "7edeb5c3-a09e-54db-bcc0-b4c0c2e9750f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7edeb5c3-a09e-54db-bcc0-b4c0c2e9750f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058029} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a8fbcc-af57-50a1-831d-d2a935a00969" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058024}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d044bebd-f049-585d-81d1-00fe08c3d294" :question/math-academy-id "q-24911" :question/problem "What is $\\arccos (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73b1caf6-3bab-5f75-b45c-f39e3bc4c897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73b1caf6-3bab-5f75-b45c-f39e3bc4c897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269013} :answer-field/id #uuid "55466596-4c56-5b42-bcc5-82815c724d32" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269012}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "054b2464-cbfe-5703-9e95-22b9dbe5aff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-277819" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=4(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cfa2701-df5c-53ae-adad-de5756a63835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269016} {:answer/id #uuid "facdade4-1ac0-5528-8663-e5c0c9808faf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "facdade4-1ac0-5528-8663-e5c0c9808faf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269017} :answer-field/id #uuid "b29375df-bc3d-53c4-bd9e-f6b1a934dd80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269015}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ffa514f-b643-5de4-904b-ef0792b95fda" :question/math-academy-id "q-320642" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\ncos\\,({120}^{∘})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\narccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea224e0a-7ad4-5010-a1a0-80811796a219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea224e0a-7ad4-5010-a1a0-80811796a219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269020} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcf6e2c9-efc7-5e22-8267-79a378a80290" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269019}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7ed2198-fcaf-5c09-8a30-b7bdff807930" :question/math-academy-id "q-320646" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf82005b-33ef-575c-949a-89c143c51948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf82005b-33ef-575c-949a-89c143c51948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269023} :answer-field/id #uuid "61f53033-bd9c-54d0-a344-513494b55726" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269022}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ce7784f-f6e3-515b-8004-feee143d1026" :question/math-academy-id "q-320721" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7f3cb27-22ab-5228-96a1-c2f3b98c731f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269026} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c7a204-1c72-5500-b0aa-a41bfc3f0c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7f3cb27-22ab-5228-96a1-c2f3b98c731f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269026} :answer-field/id #uuid "06cea244-ce37-5115-afe3-ae4fc4bb0319" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269025}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7893b97-a032-5b93-8a30-5368e64fd2a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-320893" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, 